# Code Review Comments

This file is modelled after [Go code review comments](https://go.dev/wiki/CodeReviewComments). It's not comprehensive.

## General

### Indent if flow

Try to keep the normal code path at a minimal indentation. This improves readability. Eg, return/break/continue/throw
early from an if statement.

Instead of:

```ts
if (condition) {
  console.log('condition A');
} else {
  console.log('condition B');
}
```

Do:

```ts
if (condition) {
  console.log('condition A');
  return;
}

console.log('condition B');
```

Obviously this is not always possible but more often than not you can do this. If you find yourself
needing to reach for the else too often, your function can probably be refactored.


### Composability

Keep functions/methods small and focused. Roughly guideline is 50 lines of code.

### Dependency Injection

Use dependency injection where possible. It's normally possible ~90 of the time. This
make it easier to test code. For instance:

Instead of:

```ts
function getUserEmail(userId: string) {
  const user = getUser(userId);
  const email = user.emails.find((u) => ...);
}
```

Do:

```ts
function getUserEmail(user: User) {
  const email = user.find((u) => ...);
}
```

You can even make the function arguments more minimal:

```ts
function getUserEmail(user: { emails: string[] }) {
  const email = user.emails.find((u) => ...);
}
```

### File names

Use `kebab-case` for file names (including folder and files with components).

### Args

Use `args` as the name of the argument that contains all the parameters
for a function. This makes it easier to add new parameters in the future
and also verify you're passing in the correct parameters.

```ts
function myFunction(args: { param1: string, param2: number }) {
  // ...
}

// vs the case where its easier to pass incorrect parameters

function myFunction(param1: string, param2: number) {
  // ...
}

myFunction(param1, param2) !== myFunction(param2, param1)
```

### Logging

We use structured logging. Prefer using a base message and adding fields to the object.

```ts
// Instead of
console.log(`New user ${name} signed up with email ${email}`);

// Do
logger.info(`New user signed up`, { email, name });
```

This lets you search for logs using the message and filter by fields.

## Frontend

### Handler props

The handler function should be named `handle<Event>` in the defining function and
passed down as `on<Event>` to the component.

```tsx
function MyComponent() {
  const handleClick = () => {
    console.log('clicked');
  };

  return <button onClick={handleClick}>Click me</button>;
}
```
